From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] A local light's shadow takes the sun's rays

Patch 0009 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The sun's shadow of a figure takes the Shadow rays row's count over its disc, eight by default
and sixteen on something that moves, each sample with its own blue noise. A local light's shadow
took ONE ray, and one ray at one point on a six unit disc makes the ground under him either fully
blocked or fully lit, per pixel, per frame, which is the largest variance a sample can have. One
noise value was also fetched before the loop over the lights and reused for every one of them, so
two lights at once sampled the same point on their discs and their grain moved together. And a
fairy never stops moving, so her shadow's edge sweeps ground that does not, which is the one case
the history's reject rule refuses to keep, leaving the grain nothing to average it.

It now takes the same count, each sample from its own noise, with the index carrying the light as
well as the sample. The cost is measured rather than guessed: the budget line puts this pass at
0.034 ms against the sun's 0.68 and the occlusion's 1.5, so eight rays here are about a quarter
of a millisecond.

---
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -479,7 +479,24 @@
     const float3 normal = gShadingNormal[launchIndex].xyz;
     const float3 origin = rayOrigin(position, normal);
     const float biasBase = ShadowBiasBase + ShadowBiasPerUnit * length(position - eyePosition());
-    const float3 noise = getBlueNoise(gBlueNoise, launchIndex, FrParams.frameCount + 17);
+    // A LOCAL LIGHT'S SHADOW GETS THE SAME RAYS AS THE SUN'S (2026-09-25, the user: every
+    // other shadow in the game is clean now and "navis light is still very very grainy ...
+    // her shadow under link").
+    //
+    // It had ONE ray. One ray at one point on a six unit disc makes the ground under him
+    // either fully blocked or fully lit, per pixel, per frame: the largest variance a sample
+    // can have, where the sun's shadow of the same figure takes eight and comes out smooth.
+    // Worse, ONE noise value was fetched before the loop and reused for every light in the
+    // list, so two lights at once sampled the same point on their discs and their grain moved
+    // together. Worst of all she never stops moving, so her shadow's edge sweeps ground that
+    // does not, which is the one case the history's reject rule deliberately refuses to keep,
+    // and the grain is left with nothing to average it.
+    //
+    // So: the Shadow rays row's count here too, each sample its own noise, and the index
+    // carries the light as well as the sample so two lights never share a point. The budget
+    // line puts this pass at 0.034 ms against the sun's 0.68 and the occlusion's 1.5, so
+    // eight rays here are about a quarter of a millisecond.
+    const uint lightSamples = max(uint(RtParams.tuneExperiment.z), 1u);
     float3 sum = 0.0f;
     // What the lights WOULD give this surface were nothing in the way, beside what they do give
     // (2026-09-24, the user: "torches and navi and all light types must be making [shadows]"):
@@ -506,16 +523,11 @@
         float3 tangent;
         float3 bitangent;
         tangentFrame(direction, tangent, bitangent);
-        const float angle = 6.28318531f * noise.x;
-        const float spread = sqrt(noise.y) * light.pointRadius;
-        const float3 target = light.position + tangent * (spread * cos(angle)) + bitangent * (spread * sin(angle));
         // THE RAY LEAVES THE SURFACE FURTHER AT A GRAZING ANGLE (2026-09-24). Half a unit was
         // enough while a wrong answer only cost a little added light; with a light's shadow
         // darkening the surface, a floor lit from the side by a torch shadowed itself in
         // stripes. The bias grows as the light nears the surface's plane.
         const float3 shadowOrigin = position + normal * (biasBase * (1.0f + 2.0f / max(ndl, 0.25f)));
-        const float3 toTarget = target - shadowOrigin;
-        const float targetDistance = length(toTarget);
         // The last stretch before the light is not traced: a flame sits in or on its holder (a
         // sconce, a torch stand, a lantern), and traced to the point the holder shadowed every
         // surface from its own light.
@@ -531,10 +543,24 @@
         const float clearance = ((light.groupBits & 4u) != 0u)
             ? light.pointRadius
             : max(light.pointRadius, LightClearance);
-        const float tMax = max(targetDistance - clearance, 0.0f);
-        const float visibility = (tMax > LocalBlockerSkip)
-            ? traceVisibilityRange(shadowOrigin, toTarget / targetDistance, LocalBlockerSkip, tMax, hitDistance)
-            : 1.0f;
+        // The disc sampled properly: one point per sample, each from its own blue noise, and
+        // the index carries the light as well as the sample so two lights never take the same
+        // point on their own discs.
+        float blocked = 0.0f;
+        for (uint ls = 0; ls < lightSamples; ls++) {
+            const float3 noise = getBlueNoise(gBlueNoise, launchIndex, FrParams.frameCount + 17u + ls * 31u + i * 7u);
+            const float angle = 6.28318531f * noise.x;
+            const float spread = sqrt(noise.y) * light.pointRadius;
+            const float3 target = light.position + tangent * (spread * cos(angle)) + bitangent * (spread * sin(angle));
+            const float3 toTarget = target - shadowOrigin;
+            const float targetDistance = length(toTarget);
+            const float tMax = max(targetDistance - clearance, 0.0f);
+            const float sample = (tMax > LocalBlockerSkip)
+                ? traceVisibilityRange(shadowOrigin, toTarget / targetDistance, LocalBlockerSkip, tMax, hitDistance)
+                : 1.0f;
+            blocked += 1.0f - sample;
+        }
+        const float visibility = 1.0f - (blocked / float(lightSamples));
         const float core2 = light.attenuationExponent * light.attenuationExponent;
         const float falloff = lightFalloff(distance2, radius2, core2);
         sum += light.diffuseColor * (falloff * ndl * visibility);
-- 
